#!/usr/bin/env bash
# edm-gateguard -- PreToolUse fact-forcing edit gate for Phase 6 (EDMV4-T11).
#
# Why this exists: every other EDM hook fires a handful of times per conversation. `PreToolUse`
# fires exactly once per `git commit`, where a 3,000 ms budget is acceptable because a human is
# already waiting on it. This hook fires on every Edit, Write and MultiEdit call -- tens to
# hundreds of times per Phase 6 wave -- a different order of magnitude entirely, and the reason
# every design choice below optimizes for the common case rather than the gated one:
#
#   * A repository with no active Phase 6 initiative pays exactly one process exec and one marker
#     file test. No other filesystem read happens, and no parser subprocess is ever spawned.
#   * Two rejected designs, recorded for anyone tempted to reach for them later: re-deriving "which
#     initiative is active" from a fresh sweep on every call costs one interpreter startup plus one
#     parser subprocess per active initiative (at a 50-initiative fixture, fifty subprocesses PER
#     EDIT); anything shaped like a full state checkpoint (a write lock plus a hash over every
#     tracked artifact) is Stop/PreCompact-cadence work, not per-edit work. Both are the failure
#     mode the marker file exists to avoid, not an optimization left on the table.
#
# Provenance (decisions.md D14, ratified at Gate 2): this is a from-scratch rewrite, not a port or
# a vendored copy of either upstream implementation -- see plugins/edm/CLAUDE.md's "Prompt
# conventions (house style)" section for the recorded attribution once EDMV4-T16 lands.
#
# Scope of THIS build: the marker check, the allow path, and the host registration only. Decision
# emission and its two selectable back-ends (EDMV4-T13), the fact-forcing denial content and the
# per-file MultiEdit loop (EDMV4-T14), and the operational safety controls -- kill switches,
# exemption globs, session state, the denial budget (EDMV4-T15) -- all land in follow-on tickets.
# Every call that reaches the gated path below still allows today; that is deliberate, not a bug --
# every acceptance criterion this build satisfies is independently completable without a working
# deny path, because the function that will eventually deny anything lives inside this very file
# and cannot exist before the file itself does.
#
# EDM-HELP-BEGIN
# edm-gateguard - PreToolUse fact-forcing edit gate for Phase 6 (structural build).
#
# Usage:
#   edm-gateguard              # normal invocation: reads the host's PreToolUse JSON payload from
#                               # stdin (tool_name, tool_input, ...) and never takes arguments
#   edm-gateguard -h|--help    # show this help
#
# Registration: plugins/edm/hooks/hooks.json's PreToolUse array carries a matcher block for
# Edit|Write|MultiEdit whose command guards on this binary's presence exactly like the existing
# `git commit` block does (`command -v edm-gateguard >/dev/null 2>&1 || exit 0` before exec), so an
# uninstalled or partially-installed plugin never blocks a tool call it cannot evaluate.
#
# What runs today: a repository with no active Phase 6 marker allows immediately -- one process
# exec, one marker file test, no other filesystem read, no parser subprocess. A repository WITH an
# active Phase 6 marker also allows today (the deny mechanism is a follow-on ticket's own build),
# but the gated code path is exercised so its cost is measurable ahead of that ticket landing.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0  the tool call is allowed (the only outcome this build produces on the gated path), or
#      -h/--help was requested.
#   1  a setup error (unexpected argument, an unparseable stdin payload, or a required parser
#      missing once a marker is present). A setup error never blocks the tool call -- this is the
#      same non-blocking convention every other hook in this plugin already applies (see
#      plugins/edm/CLAUDE.md's "Hooks behavior" section), and it deliberately differs from this
#      family of scripts' usual default of 2: on a PreToolUse hook, exit code 2 is how a real
#      policy refusal blocks a call, so a plain setup error here must never reuse that code by
#      accident. A later ticket's own decision-emitting function is what actually spends exit
#      code 2, and only for a real refusal, never for a setup condition.
# EDM-HELP-END
set -euo pipefail

SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}")" && pwd)"
source "${SCRIPT_DIR}/_edm-cli-lib.sh"

# die <msg> [<code>] -- see the EDM-HELP block above for why this script's own default (1) departs
# from the family-standard 2 (edm-lint-artifacts, edm-repo-readiness, edm-compare-eval): those are
# standalone tools where 2 means "usage error"; this one is a PreToolUse hook where 2 means "deny",
# so this default must never collide with that meaning.
die() {
  local msg="$1" code="${2:-1}"
  echo "edm-gateguard: $msg" >&2
  exit "$code"
}

usage() {
  print_help "${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}"
  exit 0
}

# ---- Argument parsing: no positional arguments are legal; the payload arrives on stdin. --------
# A real hook invocation from the host never passes an argument at all -- this parsing exists so
# the binary is also a well-behaved standalone CLI tool for manual testing and for this ticket's
# own smoke coverage, matching every other bin/ helper's -h/--help/help convention.
case "${1:-}" in
  -h|--help|help) usage ;;
esac

if [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; then
  die "unexpected argument: '$1' -- edm-gateguard takes no positional arguments; its payload arrives on stdin as the host's PreToolUse JSON"
fi

# ---- Shared data-directory resolver: guarded sourcing (EDMV4-T17's own contract) -- a missing or
# partially-installed library degrades this script to today's behaviour (no gate at all) rather
# than aborting or, worse, guessing at a marker path. ---------------------------------------------
if [[ -f "${SCRIPT_DIR}/_edm-datadir-lib.sh" ]]; then
  # shellcheck source=/dev/null
  source "${SCRIPT_DIR}/_edm-datadir-lib.sh"
fi

if ! declare -F edm_marker_path >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  # The resolver never loaded (missing file, or a partial install that failed to define its own
  # public function). An unresolvable environment is never a reason to block a tool call -- it is
  # a reason to behave exactly as this plugin did before this ticket existed: no gate at all.
  exit 0
fi

MARKER_PATH="$(edm_marker_path)"

# ---- The allow path: the ONLY filesystem check this script performs when no initiative is in
# Phase 6. `test -f` here is the single stat this build spends on that common case -- one process
# exec, one marker test, zero parser subprocesses, zero state reads. Nothing is written to stdout
# or stderr on this branch: silence on allow is the contract a later ticket's own decision-emission
# function inherits, not something it introduces. -------------------------------------------------
if [[ -z "$MARKER_PATH" ]] || ! test -f "$MARKER_PATH"; then
  exit 0
fi

# ===================================================================================================
# Gated path: a Phase 6 marker exists for this project. Everything below this line is the only
# place in this script where a JSON parser is a legitimate dependency (AD3's qualifier) -- nothing
# above this comment ever references one, by construction, so a repository with no active Phase 6
# initiative never spawns a parser subprocess at all.
# ===================================================================================================

command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1 \
  || die "a JSON parser is required once a Phase 6 marker is present (the allow path above needed none)"

# Read the host's PreToolUse JSON payload from stdin. `read -d ''` is a bash builtin: it reads
# until end-of-file (no literal NUL ever appears in a JSON document) with no subprocess spawned to
# do it, matching this script's own required-binary contract (bash builtins, jq, stat/test only).
PAYLOAD=""
IFS= read -r -d '' PAYLOAD || true

if [[ -z "$PAYLOAD" ]]; then
  die "no payload was delivered on stdin -- edm-gateguard expects the host's PreToolUse JSON document"
fi

if ! TOOL_NAME="$(printf '%s' "$PAYLOAD" | jq -r '.tool_name // empty' 2>/dev/null)"; then
  die "could not parse the JSON payload delivered on stdin"
fi

# ---- EDMV4-T13: the single decision-emitting function, with two selectable back-ends. Every
# deny and every allow on this gated path is emitted from here and nowhere else in this file --
# no other printf/echo prints a decision payload or a fact list, and no other line exits with a
# decision-carrying status. A later ticket's fact content (EDMV4-T14) and operational controls
# (EDMV4-T15) both call INTO this function; neither grows a second, competing decision site. -----

# EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE_DEFAULT: set from Spike B's recorded outcome (decisions.md D26, run live
# against `claude --version` 2.1.246, disposable mktemp -d repositories). The JSON
# hookSpecificOutput mechanism denied both `Edit` and `Write` cleanly, with the reason surfaced to
# the agent as a clean tool-result string; the exit-2-plus-stderr mechanism also denied both but
# leaked the hook's own shell command text into the agent's transcript. `MultiEdit` itself was
# UNTESTABLE on that host (the tool never loaded into the session's toolset) and is not evidence
# either way. This single named constant is the whole lever if that evidence is ever superseded --
# flip this one line and the smoke assertion that pins its value fails a test, not a wave; neither
# back-end below is rewritten.
EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE_DEFAULT="json"

# emit_decision <allow|deny> [<reason>] -- the ONLY place in this script that ever prints a
# decision payload or exits with a decision-carrying status (0 for allow and for a json-mode
# deny -- the JSON payload IS the deny signal the host reads; 2 for an exit-code-mode deny, the
# same code `edm-lint-staged-artifacts:7-10,150-158` already spends to mean "block"). <reason> is
# ignored for "allow": AC2 requires nothing be written to stdout on an allow, in either mode.
# EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE selects the back-end; any value other than the two legal ones below is a
# setup error (CA-298 -- a setup condition never blocks, matching this file's own `die()`
# contract) regardless of which decision was requested, so a misconfigured mode is caught the
# first time this function runs rather than only on the next real denial.
emit_decision() {
  local decision="$1"
  local reason="${2:-}"
  local mode="${EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE:-$EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE_DEFAULT}"

  case "$mode" in
    json|exit-code) : ;;
    *)
      die "EDM_GATEGUARD_DENY_MODE must be 'json' or 'exit-code' (got: '${mode}')"
      ;;
  esac

  case "$decision" in
    allow)
      exit 0
      ;;
    deny)
      case "$mode" in
        json)
          # AC8: the payload is built with `jq -cn`, never string concatenation -- a quote, a
          # backslash or an embedded newline in $reason is escaped correctly by jq's own --arg
          # handling, the exact class of bug a hand-rolled escaper would reintroduce. `-c`
          # (compact) is required: jq's default pretty-printed multi-line form would not match
          # AC2's single-line literal.
          jq -cn --arg reason "$reason" \
            '{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"PreToolUse","permissionDecision":"deny","permissionDecisionReason":$reason}}'
          exit 0
          ;;
        exit-code)
          # AC3: the fact list goes to stderr as plain text -- no JSON, so no escaping concern
          # applies to this back-end at all.
          printf '%s\n' "$reason" >&2
          exit 2
          ;;
      esac
      ;;
    *)
      die "emit_decision: unknown decision '${decision}' (expected 'allow' or 'deny')"
      ;;
  esac
}

# ---- Dispatch on tool name. Bash 3.2 floor (C1): a `case` over a small, fixed name set is the
# portable form here, not an associative array keyed by tool name -- the same idiom the fact-list
# and per-file MultiEdit loop (EDMV4-T14) will extend rather than replace. -------------------------
case "$TOOL_NAME" in
  Edit)
    # Extension point: EDMV4-T14 wires the four-fact Edit denial here, calling emit_decision deny
    # with that content. emit_decision itself (EDMV4-T13) already exists above; what is still
    # missing is the fact text and the decision of WHETHER to deny (EDMV4-T15's kill switches,
    # exemption globs and session state) -- neither is this ticket's scope.
    : ;;
  Write)
    # Extension point: EDMV4-T14 wires the swapped-fact Write denial here (name the caller, confirm
    # no existing file already serves the same purpose, in place of the Edit variant's import-
    # search facts) the same way.
    : ;;
  MultiEdit)
    # Extension point: EDMV4-T14's per-file loop iterates the batch here and denies on the first
    # still-unchecked path, returning immediately rather than evaluating the rest of the batch.
    : ;;
  *)
    # A tool name reached the gated path that this gate does not target (a future matcher change,
    # or a payload this build did not anticipate). Allow rather than guess at a decision for a
    # case nothing above named -- this gate has never inspected anything but Edit/Write/MultiEdit
    # and is not the place to start guessing about a tool it was never registered against.
    : ;;
esac

# Every branch above currently falls through to here: no ticket has yet wired a real deny
# condition into any of the three cases (that is EDMV4-T14's fact content and EDMV4-T15's
# operational controls), so this build's own behavior is still an unconditional allow -- but it is
# now emitted through the single decision-emitting function above rather than a bare `exit 0`,
# so AC1 holds even before a real denial exists anywhere in this file.
emit_decision allow ""

# =====================================================================================================
# Reference for the follow-on tickets that build on this file (not executed -- documentation only).
#
# The performance target table this build's own allow path is measured against names two rows:
#   * allow path (marker absent):            50 ms p95, one repository with no active Phase 6
#                                             initiative, over 20 samples -- `timing.sh --gateguard`.
#   * gated path (marker present, first touch): 1 exec, 2-3 stat, 1 parser invocation, 1 small
#                                             append -- the cumulative shape across every ticket that
#                                             touches this file, not a single ticket's own budget.
#
# What accrues where, so a future contributor does not re-derive the split from scratch:
#   * This ticket (T11) spends: 1 exec, 1 stat (the marker test above), 1 parser invocation (the
#     tool_name parse above) once the marker is present -- and zero of either on the allow path.
#   * EDMV4-T15's session-state file (`${data}/run/<project-key>.checked`) is where the remaining
#     1-2 stat calls (reading, then checking the state file's own mtime for the 30-minute staleness
#     rule) and the 1 small append land -- neither exists in this build, by design (Out of Scope).
#   * EDMV4-T13's decision-emitting function and EDMV4-T14's fact content are what turn today's
#     unconditional allow on the gated path into an actual first-touch denial; neither changes the
#     op-count shape above, since the marker test, the parser invocation, and the state-file work
#     are the operations the table already accounts for.
#
# None of the above is invoked from this file today. It is recorded here, rather than only in the
# ticket pack, because the next contributor to touch this file is more likely to open it than to
# re-read `srd.md` Sec.9.1 first.
#
# `bin/tests/timing.sh --gateguard` measures both branches -- marker-absent and marker-present --
# against a generated fixture, so a change to either branch's cost shows up as a real number the
# next time that mode runs, rather than as an assumption nobody re-checked.
# =====================================================================================================
